From ddd20e390011152f47da9fcb1736ee403f8532cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 15:03:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix nine things nobody had looked at
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The last pass was written on a machine with no Node and a preview surface a
few dozen pixels wide, and its own README says so: geometry and state were
asserted numerically, and "whether it *looks* right is the one thing that has
not been established". This is that pass, run against a real browser at a real
size. Nine faults, all of them visible, none of them reachable from the
numbers that were checked.

The tonearm never drew. `.deck-arm-tube` asked for `min(62%, 210px)` of a
22px pivot box, so the arm — tube, headshell and all — was 13.6px of grey
beside the spindle and the 210px ceiling never once applied. Sized off the
deck it is an arm again; the angles that put the stylus on the lead-in groove
and the run-out are re-derived for the length it actually has, because the old
pair were written for an arm that had never been seen and put the stylus off
the edge of the record at the start of a side. Dragging it ran backwards
against the paint, too: a positive CSS rotation carries the tip left, and the
pointer maths did not negate for it.

The album's back cover printed its tracklist into 4px. `.back-list` was the
only elastic block on a 232px card, so it absorbed whatever the three fixed
blocks left, and once the spec block grew past four rows that was nothing —
the tracklist's own rows spilled out of it and printed over the spec. The
tracklist is what a back cover is for, so it has a floor now and the fine
print gives way instead.

The shelf was unreadable. `.spine-text` is in a vertical writing mode, where
`flex-direction: column` runs *across* the spine: two text columns and an 8px
gap in 15px of room left each of them four pixels wide. Along the length, the
way a spine is printed, every record on the shelf names itself.

The Floor's vanishing point was off the bottom of the screen and moved with
the size of the library. `perspective-origin: 50% 34%` sat on `.floor`, whose
height is the whole scroll range, so it resolved against 2,691px of it and
drifted against the sticky frame as the page scrolled. Rows converged
downward and each one buried the titles of the row in front. On the camera the
horizon is a fixed fraction of what you can see, and the rows stand below it.

Every Look swatch ran its name into its description — "AquaWet glass and a
deep horizon" — because both are spans and neither was told to be a block.
The `margin-top` on the note, which does nothing to an inline box, is the
note that a stacked pair was always what it wanted.

The VU meter was a scatter of dust with a blue stroke through it. Its scale
was struck from a point fourteen pixels above the needle's pivot, out of
wedges .6deg wide — a fifth of a pixel at that radius. Same pivot, wide enough
to rasterise, masked to the band the needle sweeps, and the red end put on the
arc instead of in the corner.

Also: the Back button under the record lost its bottom nine pixels to the
hero's overflow; the EQ's top gridline label read "2" instead of "20k"; and
returning to a route scrolled you there over 800ms instead of restoring you,
because `#view` carries `scroll-behavior: smooth` and assigning `scrollTop`
obeys it. That last one is what the layout suite had been reporting
intermittently as a control buried under the transport — it was measuring
mid-flight.

`tools/smoke.mjs` could not have caught any of it past the halfway mark: the
visualisers hand their canvas to a worker, and reading one back with
`getContext('2d')` throws once control has been transferred. The uncaught
error ended the run at the queue panel and took the stage, the circles, the
band overview, persistence and auto-reconnect with it. Copying the canvas into
a scratch one reads the frame the worker last pushed and works on both paths.

Verified on Chromium at 1440x900 in both themes, and with the suites the
README asks for: smoke, interactions, audio and layout all pass, and layout is
clean at every width across three consecutive runs.

